WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.555 --> 00:00:02.188
- Everything I did, I
was following something

00:00:02.188 --> 00:00:03.298
I really cared about.

00:00:03.298 --> 00:00:04.567
Everybody said it was a crazy thing to do

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.988
'cause in those days, nobody
made it into the film business.

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:09.079
Everybody has talent,
and it's just a matter

00:00:09.079 --> 00:00:13.091
of moving around until
you discovered what it is.

00:00:13.091 --> 00:00:14.362
I can't make the movies to make money,

00:00:14.362 --> 00:00:15.918
I just make the movies to make them good,

00:00:15.918 --> 00:00:17.069
and if they make money, fine.

00:00:17.069 --> 00:00:18.586
I don't ever see movies by myself,

00:00:18.586 --> 00:00:19.852
I always see them with other people.

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:22.184
Most people, you know, you
don't see the struggle part

00:00:22.184 --> 00:00:24.643
of a person's life, you only
see the success they have.

00:00:24.643 --> 00:00:25.664
Yeah, okay.

00:00:25.664 --> 00:00:27.057
You're so smart, try this.

00:00:27.057 --> 00:00:29.960
Happiness is pleasure
and happiness is joy.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.450
Compassion is really
the secret to happiness.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.797
If we didn't sort of band
together, we wouldn't survive.

00:00:35.797 --> 00:00:37.989
And that, if one could make it, that one

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:39.719
would help all the others make it.

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:42.050
- He's an American
filmmaker and entrepreneur.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.419
He's best known as the
creator of the Star Wars

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:46.872
and Indiana Jones
franchises, as well as being

00:00:46.872 --> 00:00:48.389
the founder of Lucasfilm.

00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:51.254
As of March 2015, George Lucas' net worth

00:00:51.254 --> 00:00:55.707
was estimated to be $5.1
billion by Forbes magazine.

00:00:55.707 --> 00:00:57.305
He's George Lucas, and here are

00:00:57.305 --> 00:00:59.722
his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:02.313 --> 00:01:04.091
- Everything I did, I
was following something

00:01:04.091 --> 00:01:05.484
I really cared about.

00:01:05.484 --> 00:01:08.761
Something that I loved,
something I was passionate about.

00:01:08.761 --> 00:01:10.720
And I kept following that passion.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.966
Whether it was cars,
whether it was anthropology.

00:01:13.966 --> 00:01:16.633
Whether it was art, photography.

00:01:18.574 --> 00:01:22.118
And eventually, it led
me to my huge passion,

00:01:22.118 --> 00:01:23.874
my real passion, which was making movies,

00:01:23.874 --> 00:01:25.630
which combined all of those things.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:28.499
And I realized that, had
I gone on to get my degree

00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:30.583
in anthropology, I would've probably made

00:01:30.583 --> 00:01:32.637
anthropological movies in
New Guinea or some place

00:01:32.637 --> 00:01:35.829
and eventually been on National
Geographic and you know,

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:39.722
the history channel,
and been making features

00:01:39.722 --> 00:01:42.343
and I'd have done Star Wars just the same.

00:01:42.343 --> 00:01:45.259
If I'd have gone to art center
and become an illustrator,

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:47.056
I would've probably
started doing animation

00:01:47.056 --> 00:01:49.227
and doing animated films
and making animated things,

00:01:49.227 --> 00:01:50.541
and then ultimately, I would've gone on

00:01:50.541 --> 00:01:51.682
and been right where I was.

00:01:51.682 --> 00:01:54.425
So no matter which route
I took, because I cared

00:01:54.425 --> 00:01:58.270
about all of them, they
all led to the same place.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.680
Had I done what my father
had wished me to do,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.602
which was to go into the office
equipment business with him,

00:02:04.602 --> 00:02:07.510
which I knew I wasn't going
to do, I knew I hated that.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.260
My life would've been unpleasant.

00:02:12.211 --> 00:02:16.378
So I think it's very
important not to do what your

00:02:17.362 --> 00:02:21.086
peers think you should do,
not do what your parents think

00:02:21.086 --> 00:02:23.376
you should do or your teachers, but to do

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:26.495
what you inside, or even
your culture thinks,

00:02:26.495 --> 00:02:28.176
but do what's inside you.

00:02:28.176 --> 00:02:31.410
I decided to go to film school
because I loved the idea

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.461
of making films, I love photography.

00:02:34.461 --> 00:02:37.252
And everybody said it
was a crazy thing to do,

00:02:37.252 --> 00:02:39.872
'cause in those days, nobody
made it into the film business.

00:02:39.872 --> 00:02:41.633
I mean just, unless you
were related to somebody,

00:02:41.633 --> 00:02:42.784
there was no way in.

00:02:42.784 --> 00:02:44.169
So I was, you know, I was silly.

00:02:44.169 --> 00:02:46.510
You're never going to get a job.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.760
But I wasn't moved by that,

00:02:50.318 --> 00:02:54.078
I set the goal of getting
through film school.

00:02:54.078 --> 00:02:57.578
And just focused on getting to that level.

00:02:58.423 --> 00:03:01.950
'Cause I didn't know where I
was going to go after that.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.434
I wanted to make documentary films.

00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:07.002
And eventually, I got into the goal of,

00:03:07.002 --> 00:03:09.424
once I got to school, of making a film.

00:03:09.424 --> 00:03:11.930
One of the most telling
things about film school

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:14.222
is you got a lot of students,
in those days especially,

00:03:14.222 --> 00:03:16.718
it's not quite so much today,
but wandering around saying,

00:03:16.718 --> 00:03:17.946
"Oh, I wish I could make a movie,

00:03:17.946 --> 00:03:19.462
"I wish I could make a movie."

00:03:19.462 --> 00:03:22.006
You know, I can't get in
this class, I can't get into,

00:03:22.006 --> 00:03:23.685
you know, the first class I had was I had

00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:27.367
an animation class, it
wasn't a production class.

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:29.736
I had a history class
and an animation class.

00:03:29.736 --> 00:03:34.130
And in the animation class,
they gave us one minute of film

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:38.130
to put onto the animation
camera, to operate it,

00:03:38.971 --> 00:03:41.444
to see how you could
move left, move right,

00:03:41.444 --> 00:03:42.875
make it go up and down.

00:03:42.875 --> 00:03:44.958
And it was a test to see,

00:03:46.406 --> 00:03:49.046
you know, and the teacher
would add certain requirements

00:03:49.046 --> 00:03:50.273
that you had to do.

00:03:50.273 --> 00:03:51.750
You had to make it go
up and make it go down,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.259
and then the teacher
would look at it and say,

00:03:53.259 --> 00:03:57.552
"Oh yes, you maneuvered this
machine to do these things."

00:03:57.552 --> 00:04:01.328
And I took that one minute of
film and made it into a movie.

00:04:01.328 --> 00:04:05.495
And it was a movie that won
like, you know, 20, 25 awards,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.346
you know, in every film
festival in the world.

00:04:09.346 --> 00:04:14.107
And kind of changed the
whole animation department.

00:04:14.107 --> 00:04:16.088
Meanwhile, all the other guys
were running around saying,

00:04:16.088 --> 00:04:17.035
"Oh, I wish I could make a movie,

00:04:17.035 --> 00:04:18.589
"I wish I was in the production class."

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:20.020
And so then I got into another class,

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:21.942
and it wasn't really a production class,

00:04:21.942 --> 00:04:23.788
but I managed to get some
film and I made a movie,

00:04:23.788 --> 00:04:25.912
and I made lots of movies
while I was in school

00:04:25.912 --> 00:04:27.724
while everybody else was
running around saying,

00:04:27.724 --> 00:04:29.676
"Oh, I wish I could make a movie."

00:04:29.676 --> 00:04:31.724
Everybody has talent,
and it's just a matter

00:04:31.724 --> 00:04:36.661
of moving around until
you've discovered what it is.

00:04:36.661 --> 00:04:39.691
A talent is a combination
of something you love

00:04:39.691 --> 00:04:42.476
a great deal, something
you can lose yourself in,

00:04:42.476 --> 00:04:45.011
something that you start at nine o'clock.

00:04:45.011 --> 00:04:48.891
Look up from your work and
it's 10 o'clock at night.

00:04:48.891 --> 00:04:52.474
And also, something
that you have a talent,

00:04:54.184 --> 00:04:56.076
not a talent for but skills, that you have

00:04:56.076 --> 00:04:58.985
a natural ability to do very well.

00:04:58.985 --> 00:05:01.559
And usually, those two things go together.

00:05:01.559 --> 00:05:05.726
I really don't read too much
about what, you know, I don't

00:05:07.401 --> 00:05:10.060
read the reviews and I
don't read what people

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:13.275
think too much, 'cause I've
got to really make my movies

00:05:13.275 --> 00:05:15.812
the way I see them and
the way I want them to be,

00:05:15.812 --> 00:05:17.697
and then people can take them
for what they really are,

00:05:17.697 --> 00:05:19.759
they can like them or not like
them, it's their privilege.

00:05:19.759 --> 00:05:23.391
But I can't really, you
know, I can't make my movies

00:05:23.391 --> 00:05:27.141
for all the influences
that people around me,

00:05:28.802 --> 00:05:30.315
for better or worse, want.

00:05:30.315 --> 00:05:33.307
You know, I can't make
the movies to make money,

00:05:33.307 --> 00:05:34.980
I just make the movies to make them good,

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.496
and if they make money, fine.

00:05:36.496 --> 00:05:39.419
You know, I can't make them to make toys.

00:05:39.419 --> 00:05:41.941
You know, if they find
that they can make a toy

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.385
out of the movie, fine.

00:05:43.385 --> 00:05:47.722
I can't make them to be
entertaining in the way

00:05:47.722 --> 00:05:50.714
that certain critics like
them to be entertaining.

00:05:50.714 --> 00:05:53.660
You know, there's these,
Star Wars especially

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:57.139
has been created in a
very particular style.

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:00.630
And I've tried to be
faithful to that style.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:01.862
If somebody doesn't like that style,

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:04.279
it's fine, it's funny that...

00:06:05.374 --> 00:06:08.074
The one thing I did hear on
the last film that came out was

00:06:08.074 --> 00:06:09.592
something they discovered, that it was

00:06:09.592 --> 00:06:11.592
a film for young people.

00:06:13.107 --> 00:06:15.449
And that was the big complaint.

00:06:15.449 --> 00:06:17.689
Why did he make a children's film?

00:06:17.689 --> 00:06:19.010
I said, well, they've all been that.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.256
Have you ever saw the other ones?

00:06:21.256 --> 00:06:22.896
It's been that way forever,
but everybody grew up

00:06:22.896 --> 00:06:26.667
and they thought that they were
going to now see Terminator 5.

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:28.581
And I'm not making that kind of movie.

00:06:28.581 --> 00:06:31.485
I think one of the
reasons that Steven and I

00:06:31.485 --> 00:06:33.283
have been as successful as we have

00:06:33.283 --> 00:06:35.245
is 'cause we like the movies.

00:06:35.245 --> 00:06:37.911
We like to go to the
movies, we enjoy movies.

00:06:37.911 --> 00:06:41.726
And we want to make movies
like the ones we enjoy.

00:06:41.726 --> 00:06:45.114
We want to be able to
entertain the audience,

00:06:45.114 --> 00:06:47.406
we want to be able to startle
the audience, we want to be able

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:50.664
to blow the audience away
and say, have them walk out

00:06:50.664 --> 00:06:52.292
of the theater saying,
"Whoa, that was fantastic,

00:06:52.292 --> 00:06:54.709
"I was really moved by that."

00:06:57.706 --> 00:07:00.706
That's what's part of the fun of it.

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:05.052
You want people to think, you want people

00:07:05.052 --> 00:07:07.265
to be emotionally moved.

00:07:07.265 --> 00:07:10.015
And there's a theory behind that.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:13.773
In terms of storytelling, it's been around

00:07:13.773 --> 00:07:16.068
for thousands of years.

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:19.218
And that's where something
like live theater

00:07:19.218 --> 00:07:23.147
or live performance is
something that is very valuable.

00:07:23.147 --> 00:07:26.497
Because you get instant
feedback from your audience,

00:07:26.497 --> 00:07:28.788
and you kind of know the things that work

00:07:28.788 --> 00:07:30.993
and the things that don't work.

00:07:30.993 --> 00:07:33.696
That's the advantage that, you know,

00:07:33.696 --> 00:07:37.491
the Greek storytellers
had and Shakespeare had

00:07:37.491 --> 00:07:42.248
that us in the film industry
are, it's harder to come by.

00:07:42.248 --> 00:07:45.692
Which is, you know, to be able
to see an audience reaction

00:07:45.692 --> 00:07:47.379
and then adjust to what works.

00:07:47.379 --> 00:07:49.753
So you have to use your
experience of sitting through

00:07:49.753 --> 00:07:50.898
a lot of movies.

00:07:50.898 --> 00:07:52.293
I don't ever see movies by myself,

00:07:52.293 --> 00:07:54.392
I always see them with other people.

00:07:54.392 --> 00:07:56.234
Because I want to know
what works, I want to know

00:07:56.234 --> 00:07:57.829
where they laugh, I want to
know where they don't laugh,

00:07:57.829 --> 00:08:00.085
I want to know what they
think about it afterwards.

00:08:00.085 --> 00:08:04.836
'Cause in the end, that's the
art that I'm working with is,

00:08:04.836 --> 00:08:06.325
you know, trying to communicate it

00:08:06.325 --> 00:08:10.866
in a way that is effective
and people react to.

00:08:10.866 --> 00:08:14.449
No matter how easy it
looks on the outside,

00:08:15.407 --> 00:08:18.490
it is a very very difficult struggle.

00:08:19.335 --> 00:08:21.009
Most people, you know, you don't see

00:08:21.009 --> 00:08:22.566
the struggle part of a person's life,

00:08:22.566 --> 00:08:25.348
you only see the success they have.

00:08:25.348 --> 00:08:27.848
But I haven't met anybody
here at the academy

00:08:27.848 --> 00:08:30.227
or anywhere else that hasn't been able

00:08:30.227 --> 00:08:32.028
to describe years and years and years of

00:08:32.028 --> 00:08:36.195
very very difficult struggle
through the whole process of,

00:08:38.605 --> 00:08:42.446
you know, achieving anything whatsoever.

00:08:42.446 --> 00:08:45.613
And there's no way to get around that.

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:49.116
The secret is just not to give up hope.

00:08:49.116 --> 00:08:51.623
It's very hard not to because,

00:08:51.623 --> 00:08:55.790
if you're really doing
something worthwhile, I think,

00:08:56.791 --> 00:09:01.422
I think you will be pushed
to the brink of hopelessness

00:09:01.422 --> 00:09:03.755
before you come through to the other side.

00:09:03.755 --> 00:09:05.676
And you just have to hang in through that.

00:09:05.676 --> 00:09:08.745
Well, I think ultimately,
we don't have to be mean.

00:09:08.745 --> 00:09:10.179
You know, we're not
really that competitive,

00:09:10.179 --> 00:09:13.257
I came out of film school
with a lot of friends

00:09:13.257 --> 00:09:15.178
and we all helped each
other, we helped each others

00:09:15.178 --> 00:09:16.689
in our careers.

00:09:16.689 --> 00:09:18.665
And we just never were
competitive about anything,

00:09:18.665 --> 00:09:21.649
we were always competitive
in a friendly way to say,

00:09:21.649 --> 00:09:24.349
you know, like playing cards or, you know,

00:09:24.349 --> 00:09:28.605
dunking baskets, to say okay,
you're so smart, try this.

00:09:28.605 --> 00:09:29.438
You know.

00:09:29.438 --> 00:09:32.418
And so it was that kind
of competition, not the,

00:09:32.418 --> 00:09:34.562
you know, you must be destroyed

00:09:34.562 --> 00:09:37.641
and you must fail so I can win
and all that sort of thing.

00:09:37.641 --> 00:09:41.368
Happiness is pleasure
and happiness is joy.

00:09:41.368 --> 00:09:43.009
You know, it can be either one.

00:09:43.009 --> 00:09:44.442
You add them up and it sort of falls under

00:09:44.442 --> 00:09:46.695
the uber category of happiness.

00:09:46.695 --> 00:09:49.521
Pleasure is short lived.

00:09:49.521 --> 00:09:53.521
It lasts an hour, lasts
a minute, lasts a month.

00:09:54.816 --> 00:09:58.647
And it peaks and then goes
down, it peaks very high.

00:09:58.647 --> 00:10:00.610
But the next time you want
to get that same peak,

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.701
you have to do it twice as much.

00:10:02.701 --> 00:10:04.258
You know, it's like
drugs, you know, you have

00:10:04.258 --> 00:10:06.430
to keep doing it 'cause
it insulates itself.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:08.600
No matter what it is,
whether you're shopping,

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.625
whether you're, you know, engaged

00:10:11.625 --> 00:10:13.833
in any other kind of pleasure.

00:10:13.833 --> 00:10:16.159
That all has the same quality about it.

00:10:16.159 --> 00:10:18.245
On the other hand is joy.

00:10:18.245 --> 00:10:22.382
And joy is the thing that
doesn't go as high as pleasure

00:10:22.382 --> 00:10:24.554
in terms of your emotional reaction.

00:10:24.554 --> 00:10:26.304
But it says with you.

00:10:27.617 --> 00:10:30.284
Joy is something you can recall.

00:10:31.143 --> 00:10:32.726
Pleasure you can't.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:38.555
The secret is that even
though it's not as intense

00:10:38.555 --> 00:10:43.481
as the pleasure, the joy
will last you a lot longer.

00:10:43.481 --> 00:10:46.054
And people who get the pleasure,
they keep saying, well,

00:10:46.054 --> 00:10:48.636
if I can just get richer
and get more cars, you know,

00:10:48.636 --> 00:10:52.508
you'll never relive the
moment you got your first car.

00:10:52.508 --> 00:10:54.110
That's it, that's the highest peak.

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:58.838
Yes, you can get three Ferraris
and a new Gulfstream jet

00:10:58.838 --> 00:11:00.760
and maybe you'll get close.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.377
But you have to keep going
and eventually you run out.

00:11:03.377 --> 00:11:05.140
I mean, you just can't
do it, doesn't work.

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:08.089
So if you're trying to sustain that level

00:11:08.089 --> 00:11:11.199
of peak pleasure, you're doomed.

00:11:11.199 --> 00:11:14.056
It's a very American idea,
but it just can't happen,

00:11:14.056 --> 00:11:15.320
you just let it go.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.856
Peak, great, pleasure is fun, it's great.

00:11:17.856 --> 00:11:19.526
But you can't keep it going forever.

00:11:19.526 --> 00:11:21.574
Just accept the fact that
it's here and it's gone.

00:11:21.574 --> 00:11:23.133
And maybe again it'll come back and then

00:11:23.133 --> 00:11:24.629
we get to do it again.

00:11:24.629 --> 00:11:25.852
Joy lasts forever.

00:11:25.852 --> 00:11:28.289
Pleasure is purely self-centered.

00:11:28.289 --> 00:11:31.890
It's all about your
pleasure, it's about you.

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:36.187
It's about, it's a selfish,
self-centered emotion

00:11:36.187 --> 00:11:40.162
that's created by a
self-centered motive of greed.

00:11:40.162 --> 00:11:42.125
Joy is compassion.

00:11:42.125 --> 00:11:46.639
Joy is giving yourself to
somebody else or something else.

00:11:46.639 --> 00:11:50.206
And it's a kind of thing
that is, in its subtlety

00:11:50.206 --> 00:11:53.071
and lowness much more
powerful than pleasure.

00:11:53.071 --> 00:11:55.402
If you get hung up on
pleasure, you're doomed,

00:11:55.402 --> 00:11:59.569
if you pursue joy, you will
find everlasting happiness.

00:12:01.043 --> 00:12:03.287
Children teach the most
important thing of all,

00:12:03.287 --> 00:12:04.954
which is compassion.

00:12:06.078 --> 00:12:10.311
And compassion is really
the secret to happiness

00:12:10.311 --> 00:12:12.807
and the secret to a good life.

00:12:12.807 --> 00:12:14.769
And it's a hard thing to learn.

00:12:14.769 --> 00:12:16.408
It's hard to learn not
to think about yourself

00:12:16.408 --> 00:12:18.356
and to think about somebody else.

00:12:18.356 --> 00:12:21.119
But when you have a little baby,

00:12:21.119 --> 00:12:24.378
it becomes pretty easy
if you're open to it.

00:12:24.378 --> 00:12:28.461
And any parent that is
reasonably normal and open

00:12:29.327 --> 00:12:32.557
to these sort of things
really does learn compassion,

00:12:32.557 --> 00:12:35.950
caring about another
person through having kids.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:39.591
And putting that other person first.

00:12:39.591 --> 00:12:42.504
I guess the advantage
that my generation has,

00:12:42.504 --> 00:12:44.180
when we were in film
school and we were starting

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.519
the film business, the
door was absolute locked.

00:12:46.519 --> 00:12:48.439
And it was a very very high wall.

00:12:48.439 --> 00:12:50.234
And nobody got in.

00:12:50.234 --> 00:12:53.846
Therefore, all of us
beggars and scroungers

00:12:53.846 --> 00:12:56.846
down at the front gate decided that,

00:12:58.189 --> 00:13:02.158
if we didn't sort of band
together, we wouldn't survive.

00:13:02.158 --> 00:13:04.289
And that, if one could make it, that one

00:13:04.289 --> 00:13:05.916
would help all the others make it,

00:13:05.916 --> 00:13:07.766
and we will continue to help each other.

00:13:07.766 --> 00:13:08.792
So we banded together.

00:13:08.792 --> 00:13:12.766
I mean, that's how cavemen figured it out.

00:13:12.766 --> 00:13:15.760
Any society starts that
way, any society begins

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:18.706
by realizing that together,
by helping each other,

00:13:18.706 --> 00:13:22.108
you can survive better than
if you fight each other

00:13:22.108 --> 00:13:23.575
and compete with each other.

00:13:23.575 --> 00:13:25.667
If I got a job, I would help
somebody else get a job,

00:13:25.667 --> 00:13:27.552
if somebody got more successful than me,

00:13:27.552 --> 00:13:28.946
it was partly my success.

00:13:28.946 --> 00:13:31.932
I wasn't, my success wasn't based on how

00:13:31.932 --> 00:13:34.263
I could push down everybody
that was around me.

00:13:34.263 --> 00:13:37.791
My success was based on how
much I could push everybody up.

00:13:37.791 --> 00:13:40.252
And eventually, their
success was the same way,

00:13:40.252 --> 00:13:43.276
and in the process, they pushed
me up and I pushed them up.

00:13:43.276 --> 00:13:44.460
And we kept doing that.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:45.364
And we still do that.

00:13:45.364 --> 00:13:48.059
And even though we all have, in essence,

00:13:48.059 --> 00:13:52.226
competing companies, we see
it as if everybody succeeds

00:13:53.628 --> 00:13:56.087
or my friend succeeds,
then everybody succeeds.

00:13:56.087 --> 00:13:58.230
And that's the key to it, is
to have everybody succeed,

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:00.987
not to gloat over somebody else's failure.

00:14:00.987 --> 00:14:02.710
One of the basic motifs of fairly tales

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.529
is that you find the poor
unfortunate alongside the road.

00:14:05.529 --> 00:14:09.332
And when they beg for help,
if you give it to them,

00:14:09.332 --> 00:14:10.395
you end up succeeding.

00:14:10.395 --> 00:14:12.359
If you don't give it to them, you end up

00:14:12.359 --> 00:14:14.284
being turned into a frog or something.

00:14:14.284 --> 00:14:17.567
So it's something that's been
around for thousands of years,

00:14:17.567 --> 00:14:19.983
a concept that's been around
for thousands of years.

00:14:19.983 --> 00:14:23.181
And it is even more necessary today

00:14:23.181 --> 00:14:27.348
when people are much more
into their own aggrandizement

00:14:28.789 --> 00:14:31.660
than they are in helping
other people, I mean,

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:34.495
the one thing that you
hear at the academy here

00:14:34.495 --> 00:14:38.550
is constantly about public
service, about helping others.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:41.169
I don't think there's anybody
who's become successful

00:14:41.169 --> 00:14:44.154
that doesn't understand
how important it is

00:14:44.154 --> 00:14:46.978
to be part of a larger
community, to help other people

00:14:46.978 --> 00:14:50.908
in the larger community, to
give back to the community.

00:14:50.908 --> 00:14:54.388
And it's not something
you do, you start doing

00:14:54.388 --> 00:14:55.619
when you've made it.

00:14:55.619 --> 00:14:59.619
Now I'm on the top, I can
sort of enhance my joy

00:15:00.573 --> 00:15:04.016
and self-esteem by helping
the poor underlings.

00:15:04.016 --> 00:15:07.855
It's when you are at the very lowest level

00:15:07.855 --> 00:15:10.319
and you're struggling, when
we were in film school,

00:15:10.319 --> 00:15:11.992
we were all very very poor, we were all

00:15:11.992 --> 00:15:15.512
very very struggling, we all
need jobs very desperately.

00:15:15.512 --> 00:15:17.475
And if one of us couldn't
get a particular job,

00:15:17.475 --> 00:15:20.347
we'd send another friend
in on the interview.

00:15:20.347 --> 00:15:24.917
Because we were hoping that
one of us would get the job.

00:15:24.917 --> 00:15:27.593
So you do it right from
the very beginning.

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:30.426
You can start every single day.

00:15:30.426 --> 00:15:33.556
You know, whether it's
helping your brother or sister

00:15:33.556 --> 00:15:37.723
or helping your peers at school
or helping in the community,

00:15:39.046 --> 00:15:42.367
but it's not just, it's not just a kind of

00:15:42.367 --> 00:15:44.655
public service thing, it's a way of life.

00:15:44.655 --> 00:15:46.374
If America is the pursuit of happiness,

00:15:46.374 --> 00:15:48.960
the best way to pursue happiness
is to help other people

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:51.860
'cause there's nothing else
that'll make you happy.

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:54.152
You can be as rich and famous and powerful

00:15:54.152 --> 00:15:56.655
as you want to be and it
will not bring you happiness.

00:15:56.655 --> 00:15:58.168
And that's said over
and over and over again

00:15:58.168 --> 00:16:01.169
and it's such a cliche
that this hardly goes,

00:16:01.169 --> 00:16:03.385
it hardly needs to be said,
but people don't understand

00:16:03.385 --> 00:16:05.385
that it's actually true.

00:16:07.184 --> 00:16:11.093
You can find people rich,
powerful and famous.

00:16:11.093 --> 00:16:12.926
And they aren't happy.

00:16:14.732 --> 00:16:17.102
And you can find people who
have discovered the fact

00:16:17.102 --> 00:16:19.485
that it's really helping
people, it's really

00:16:19.485 --> 00:16:22.096
being compassionate
toward other human beings

00:16:22.096 --> 00:16:23.846
that makes you happy.

00:16:26.405 --> 00:16:27.546
- Thank you so much for watching,

00:16:27.546 --> 00:16:30.970
I made this video because
Kevlar Blast Shield asked me to.

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:33.153
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:16:33.153 --> 00:16:35.323
to profile next, leave
in the comments below

00:16:35.323 --> 00:16:36.671
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:36.671 --> 00:16:40.365
I'd also love to know which
of George Lucas' top 10 rules

00:16:40.365 --> 00:16:41.838
meant the most to you.

00:16:41.838 --> 00:16:43.922
Leave in the comments, I'll
join in the discussion.

00:16:43.922 --> 00:16:46.953
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe,

00:16:46.953 --> 00:16:48.786
and I'll see you soon.

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:56.819
- If there's one thing
that you would like to be

00:16:56.819 --> 00:17:00.580
remembered for, one
project that you have done

00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:04.330
or that you have yet to
do, what would it be?

00:17:05.786 --> 00:17:07.286
- Raising my kids.

00:17:08.198 --> 00:17:09.804
- There you go.

00:17:09.804 --> 00:17:12.033
That's a human being right there, folks.

00:17:12.033 --> 00:17:14.248
- Learning to make films is very easy.

00:17:14.248 --> 00:17:16.948
Learning what to make
films about is very hard.

00:17:16.948 --> 00:17:19.893
And what you really got
to do is focus on learning

00:17:19.893 --> 00:17:24.060
as much about life and about
the various aspects of it

00:17:26.558 --> 00:17:30.330
first, and then learn just the
techniques of making a movie.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.637
Because that stuff you can
pick up pretty quickly.

00:17:32.637 --> 00:17:36.223
But having a really good
understanding of history,

00:17:36.223 --> 00:17:38.890
literature, psychology, sciences

00:17:41.186 --> 00:17:42.901
are very very important to actually

00:17:42.901 --> 00:17:45.527
being able to make movies.

00:17:45.527 --> 00:17:48.559
For all of my friends who are successful,

00:17:48.559 --> 00:17:51.546
all the same age, all had
school together, all had fun.

00:17:51.546 --> 00:17:52.996
The secret ingredient which everyone goes,

00:17:52.996 --> 00:17:54.059
"Why are you guys so successful?"

00:17:54.059 --> 00:17:55.570
We all love movies.

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:57.406
We aren't doing it to get rich,

00:17:57.406 --> 00:17:59.734
we never thought we were
ever going to get rich.

00:17:59.734 --> 00:18:01.949
Or be successful, or
anybody would even like it.

00:18:01.949 --> 00:18:04.599
But we love movies, and
we make the kind of movies

00:18:04.599 --> 00:18:06.439
that we'd love to see.

00:18:06.439 --> 00:18:07.551
Oh, I'd like to see that.

00:18:07.551 --> 00:18:08.696
And we have the advantage of being able

00:18:08.696 --> 00:18:10.169
to actually make the movie.

00:18:10.169 --> 00:18:12.502
You know, we'll say, "Okay,
well, it's not coming out,

00:18:12.502 --> 00:18:13.813
"so let's just make it.

00:18:13.813 --> 00:18:14.770
"Then we'll bring it out."

